# CHAPTER ONE
## A QUIET KIND OF EMPEROR
### Part Two of Three

---

She got two sentences in.

"The Galactic Alliance recognizes," Jaina said, "that the situation in the eastern reaches has moved beyond what can be managed by standoff. There are seven sectors under your administration. There are four more in dispute. The Senate is not prepared to —"

The side door hissed open for the third time.

Saska, the aide, took one step into the room and stopped. She did not look at Maerko's face. She looked, very deliberately, at his hands on the table. It was the look of a subordinate who had been trained to deliver bad news in a way that did not make her boss feel cornered. Jaina noted the training. She noted, more, the fact that Saska had been given the room's attention without asking for it.

Maerko held up one finger to Jaina. *Hold.* He did not say it.

"Saska."

"Lord. The site superintendent on the line. He says he won't sign off until you walk it personally."

"He's the superintendent. He signs off. That's what supes do."

"He says he won't, lord."

"Tell him he will."

"I have, lord. Twice."

Maerko closed his eyes for a half-beat. Not for long. Long enough that Jaina, watching his face, could see the small short-tempered thing he was choosing not to do. He opened his eyes.

"He's right," he said, mostly to himself. "Damn it. He's right."

He looked back at Jaina. The frown had moved again. It was not directed at her. It was the look of a man who had just been reminded that the universe did not, in fact, arrange itself around his calendar.

"I have to go," he said.

"All right."

"I'm sorry. This is bad form. I know it. I'll make it up to you." He glanced at Saska. "How long do I need."

"An hour, lord. Maybe seventy minutes."

"An hour." He grimaced. "An *hour*. Right. Right."

He stood. He did not stand the way Imperials stood when they were leaving a meeting. He stood the way a foreman stood when he had to go yell at someone two floors down. He looked at the four of them. He spread his hands.

"Saska is going to look after you. You can wait in here if you want. You can wait in the small reception two doors down — it's nicer, better light. You can walk the building. Anywhere your escort can take you, you can go. If you get bored, ask Saska, she'll find you something. I'd say nap, but you won't, you're not the napping kind. I'll be back in an hour. I will be back in an *hour*." He pointed at Saska. "Tell the superintendent if I'm not back by sixteen-thirty he is, personally, going to be the next person on a spike."

"Yes, lord."

"That was a joke, Saska."

"I will represent it as a joke, lord."

He looked at Jaina. His mouth pulled, the not-quite-smile.

"She does it on purpose."

"She seems good at her job."

"She is. Very. All right. An hour. Don't go anywhere I'd regret."

He was out the door before any of them had said another word.

---

For about six seconds, the room was perfectly silent.

Jag was the first to move. He drew breath, slow, and let it out through his teeth in the small whistle he made when he had just made a decision he did not like. He set his cup down. He did not look at Jaina. He did not need to.

Saska, by the windows, folded her hands in front of her at the waist.

"There's a viewing terrace on the upper floor," she said. "The city is quite nice from up there. There's also a reading room one level down. There are biscuits in both."

"Biscuits," Voss said, almost to himself.

"There are biscuits."

Jaina stood. Her knees ached, a small, surprising ache; she had been more wound-tight in that chair than she had let herself feel. She rolled her shoulders once, very slightly, and the cracking along her left scapula was loud in the quiet room.

"The terrace, please," she said.

"Of course." Saska inclined her head. "If you'll follow me."

---

The corridor was the same corridor as before and not the same corridor at all.

The light had shifted by maybe ten minutes' worth of afternoon. The slant of sun through the tall windows had moved across the polished floor and now lit the lower halves of the dark wood panels in a soft gold the color of a Corellian autumn. The protocol droid was gone from the side counter. A woman in dark grey-and-red had taken its place; she was reading a flimsiplast at arm's length the way people read who needed reading lenses and had not, yet, gotten around to ordering them. She did not look up.

The same brisk traffic moved past them in both directions. A clerk with a stack of permit forms. A pair of low-rank army officers conferring about something to do with grain ration distribution, of all things. A maintenance tech in coveralls dragging a kit-cart whose left wheel squeaked. Saska said *afternoon* to each of them as she passed, low and pleasant, and they each said *afternoon* back the way you said *afternoon* to a person you saw twice a day every day and would see twice a day every day until one of you died.

Jaina walked half a step behind her. Jag fell in at her left. Master Bel walked at her right, hands clasped behind his back in the old Praxeum way. Voss took up the rear and was, in that order, almost invisible.

They went up two short flights of broad shallow stairs and through a small landing and through a door that opened on its own as Saska approached.

The terrace was — once more, infuriatingly — not what she had been ready for.

It was a long open balcony, perhaps thirty meters by ten, paved in the same pale grey stone as the courtyard below. It was railed at chest-height in a simple wrought black metal that had not been cast in any pattern designed to impress anyone. There were eight benches along the inner wall. There were two small potted broadleafs in plain ceramic. There was, at the far end, a single round table with four chairs and a fresh teapot under a quilted insulator. A second tray held biscuits. A third tray held cups, water, and a sliced fruit she did not recognize on first glance and, on second glance, recognized as Hapan ginthel — a delicate fruit that did not travel and did not store and did not, in general, exist outside its native hemisphere.

She filed *ginthel* alongside *scrubbers* and *biscuits* and *the chairs in the office* and let the file get heavier.

"Please," Saska said, gesturing at the table. "I'll be at the door. Call me if you need anything."

She withdrew. The terrace door slid shut behind her with the soft hiss. They were, by every appearance, alone.

They were not alone. Jaina knew it the way you knew a draft was on you without seeing the window. Somewhere in the railing or the floor or the underside of the benches, audio pickups would be running. Somewhere, the null field that ran through the walls below would extend, in some thinner form, up here too. She tested the air with the back of her senses. The Force came back to her — *more* of it than below — but with a small flat resistance underneath, the texture of a thing being passively monitored.

She did not look up at the sky. She did not need to. She knew what she would see.

"Master Bel," she said.

"Yes."

"Tell me what you have."

Bel walked to the railing. He set both hands on it. He looked out over the city. The light was honey on the smelter stacks at the far horizon, on the new towers, on the old town, on a long stretch of cargo-rail that ran like a polished wire from a freight depot at the eastern edge to the central spire below them. He did not speak immediately.

He did finally.

"He is competent," Bel said.

"Yes."

"He is more than competent. The aide. The biscuits. The way he handles his own staff. The way he stood up." Bel paused. "The way he sat down. He is a man who is not playing a part. He is a man who is doing his job, and his job happens to be very large."

"Yes."

"He believes the things he said."

"Yes."

"That is the part I am having difficulty with."

Jag had drifted to the railing on Bel's other side. His face was set into the particular blank attention he used when his Chiss training was running underneath his New Republic surface; the result was a man who looked relaxed and was, in fact, cataloguing escape routes from the third floor of a foreign capital.

"He's read us," Jag said.

"How."

"He knew which of us was the polite one." Jag's eyes moved across the city. "He knew which of us was the one to make a joke at. He looked at Voss for half a beat longer than he needed to, which means he knew Voss was Intelligence before Voss sat down. He spent the most time looking at *you*, Jaina, and not the way most men look at you."

"How most men look at me?"

"He looked at you the way you look at a piece of equipment. He wasn't checking you out. He was reading the make and model."

Bel's mouth thinned.

"That fits," Bel said.

"What fits?" Voss asked, very quietly. He had not moved from the inner wall. He stood with one shoulder against it the way a man stood when he had decided to keep one side of himself in a corner.

"The room," Bel said. "The chairs. The aide. The way the door opens on its own when she walks toward it. The man does not waste resources on decoration. He spends them on function. The chairs were good because his back hurts and his guests' backs will hurt if they sit long enough. The fruit on this terrace is Hapan ginthel because he has trade with the Hapes Cluster on a route that allows perishables. The biscuits are baked locally because his fuel-import lines are good enough to support fresh-bake. The aide reads his orders before he gives them because he selects for that and trains them up. And the apologies are not, as I suspect Colonel Voss is now considering, sarcasm." Bel turned his head a little. "Are they, Colonel."

Voss took a breath. He let it out.

"No," he said. "I don't think they are."

"Which means," Bel said, "the man means it when he says he wants to do business. Which means the man is dangerous in the way a man is dangerous when he has decided what he wants and has the means to take it."

"And the atrocities?" Jaina said.

The word fell into the quiet of the terrace and lay there for a moment without anyone picking it up.

"The atrocities," Bel said eventually, "are policy. Not pleasure. I don't think he enjoys them the way the file says he does. I think he wants the file to say he does."

"That's an awfully generous reading."

"It is not generous. It is worse." Bel's hands tightened a fraction on the railing. "A man who tortures because he enjoys it has a limit. He gets tired. His arm gets tired. His stomach turns. He stops when his appetite stops. A man who tortures because it works does not stop until it stops working."

A long silence.

Jag broke it. "We need to leave that building alive."

"We will," Bel said.

"And we need to leave it with Mirella."

"That," Bel said, "I am less sure of."

Jaina did not look at any of them. She had been looking out at the city the whole time. From the terrace railing she could see, perhaps three kilometers off, the long low complex of a freight yard where a string of repulsor-trucks was being loaded by a pair of cargo droids the size of small buildings. Beyond the freight yard, in the haze of the late afternoon, a launch tower at the edge of the city was venting white steam in three rhythmic puffs — a kessel-pattern launch checkout, a thing you did before lifting heavy cargo to orbit. She had not seen one in three years.

He had a working orbital tether somewhere. He had freight droids that big. He had Hapan trade. He had a town hall that ran on three shifts.

He had been here, building this, for less than two years.

"What was here before him?" she asked quietly.

"Mining camps," Jag said. "Three of them. A garrison. A couple of failing refineries."

"And the staff."

"Locals. Some Imperial. Some refugee."

"All these people in this building. Where did they come from?"

Jag thought about it.

"He hired them," he said finally. "And not all of them are Imperial."

"No," Jaina said. "I noticed."

She had. The faces in the corridors had been a mix she would not have expected on an Imperial Remnant world. Human, mostly, but not all. She had seen a Bothan in the corridor — a *Bothan*, in a stormtrooper-style capital. She had seen a Twi'lek woman at the reception counter. She had seen a Sullustan technician at the side desk in the antechamber. She had not, in any of these cases, seen a collar or a brand or the small dead-eyed compliance of someone who had been broken into work.

These were employees.

She made herself say the next thing out loud, because she had been thinking it for ten minutes and was tired of carrying it.

"He's better at this," she said, "than the Empire ever was."

Voss made a sound that was almost a laugh and was definitely not a laugh.

"That," he said, "is the report I am not looking forward to writing."

---

Three floors down and one corridor over, Mirella Tarsh felt her stomach turn over.

She had been carrying a tea tray.

She had been carrying tea trays for a hundred and seventy-eight standard days. She knew the corridors by their sound. She knew which floor tile by the kitchen door was loose and rocked under her left foot if she stepped on it wrong. She knew which guards looked at her and which guards looked through her and which of the two she preferred at any given time. She knew the smell of the building in the morning and the smell of it in the evening and she knew the way the smelter smoke rolled in through the air-recyclers at dusk and made the upper corridors taste, very faintly, of iron.

She knew, at all times, where in the building Maerko was.

It was a thing she had figured out in the third month. He carried a particular weight in the Force. Not dark — he wasn't dark, the way her teachers had used the word — but heavy. A small dense thing, like the gravity of a planet smaller than it ought to be. She could feel that gravity at all times, the way you could feel the angle of a hill under your feet even with your eyes closed. She had not told him she could do this. She had assumed, on his behalf, that he would not like it.

The collar at her throat had two settings. *Off* meant she could touch the Force. *On* meant she could not. The setting was controlled, she had learned by careful experiment, from a small ring he wore on his right hand. He left it *off* almost all of the time. *Off* meant she could be useful to him. *Off* also meant she could, theoretically, kill him.

She had not killed him.

The reasons were complicated and she preferred not to think about them at the moment.

What had stopped her in the corridor today, between the lower service kitchen and the upper antechamber, was a different shape in the Force entirely.

It was — and she set the tray down very carefully on a side console before her hands betrayed her — *Jaina.*

She did not let any expression move across her face. She had learned, after the first month, to keep her face still even when she was alone, because alone was almost always less alone than she thought. She drew breath. She held it for a count of three. She let it out.

Master Solo was in the building.

Master Solo, who had taught her sabre forms in the upper Praxeum hall for two years before the Order had reorganized. Master Solo, who had cried — and Mirella had pretended not to see — at her own Knighting. Master Solo, whose laugh was, on the rare occasions Mirella had ever heard it in full, the loudest thing in any room. Master Solo was in this building.

She knew, with the small clean certainty of a Jedi who had spent half a year being very careful about what she let herself hope for, that Master Solo would not be here for her alone. Maerko had said, in front of her, two weeks ago, that he was negotiating with the Alliance. He had said it without looking at her, the way he said most things to her that he wanted her to hear without acknowledging that he was saying them to her. *The Alliance is sending its people. We'll see what they're made of.*

So the people had come. And one of them was Master Solo.

She picked the tray back up.

She did not let her hands shake. She did not let her breath move out of pattern. She did not — and this was the harder part — let the small ember of joy in her chest reach the surface of her face. She had been keeping that ember banked for one hundred and seventy-eight days. She could keep it banked for one more afternoon.

She walked.

The corridor was the corridor she always walked: the broad service hall on the second floor, paneled in the same dark wood, lit by the same yellow ceiling panels, busy at this hour with the same low traffic. A pair of Imperial-style officers nodded at her as she passed; she did not nod back, because nodding back was not in her job description and she had been beaten the second week for forgetting. A clerk did not look at her at all; she was furniture to him; she preferred that. A gardener with dirt on his knees did look at her, and gave her a small private smile, the kind one shaved-head prisoner gave another shaved-head prisoner in passing. She did not return that one either, but she did, very slightly, slow.

She reached the small kitchen-pantry at the end of the corridor. She set the tray down on the prep counter. She checked the pot — still warm, still steeped. She checked the cup count — six, more than she would need, but the kitchen had a rule about always sending up six. She checked her reflection in the polished metal of the warming cabinet, automatically, because they docked her if her robes were dirty.

She looked at the reflection longer than she usually did.

A young woman, shaved at the scalp, in undyed grey robes that were not Jedi robes but had been cut to a Jedi pattern by someone with a sense of humor. The collar around her throat was thin, almost decorative. Her face had thinned. There were small dark hollows under her eyes that had not been there six months ago, and a scar she had never had before along the angle of her jaw on the right, a thin pale line where a blaster bolt had grazed her in the last hour of the trap on the Hydian Way.

She had not, in six months, looked at herself this long. There had not been a point.

She looked away.

She picked up the tray.

She walked toward the back service stair that led to the upper floors. She did not, on this trip, go up. She stopped at the landing where the service stair met the main stair. She set the tray on the side console. She waited.

She knew Maerko's schedule. She knew it because she had taught herself to know it, the way a prisoner taught herself anything that might one day matter. He had been pulled out of his meeting by the depot situation forty minutes ago. He would be back in another twenty. He would come up this stair, not the lift, because his bad knee was better on stairs than in the lift and because he liked, when he was angry, to climb.

He would be angry when he came up.

She had a tea tray.

She had been instructed, on three separate prior occasions, to bring the tea up when she felt that the moment was right. He had used those words. *When you feel it's right.* She had thought, the first time, that he was setting her up to fail. He had not been. He had been delegating.

She closed her eyes for a half-second.

She could feel Master Solo, three floors above. She could feel Master Bel — that was an *enormous* shock, she had not known he was alive — and a Galactic Alliance officer who burned with the small unhappy fluorescence of an Intelligence career, and the man she assumed was Lord Fel, who burned, oddly, in two colors at once, like a banked fire under a kettle.

They would be brought back down soon. Maerko would come up first. He would want to put himself back in the office before they returned. He would want them to walk in and find him already there, calm, working a flimsi, as if the last hour had not been a thing.

He would want tea.

Mirella picked up the tray.

She started up the stairs.

---

He came in fifty-four minutes after he had left.

Jaina was back in the office before he was. So were the others. Saska had appeared on the terrace at the forty-five-minute mark and asked them, with the same polite efficiency as before, whether they would like to be returned to the inner room or whether they would prefer to be received elsewhere. They had returned. The teacups had been replaced. The light through the tall windows had moved another finger's width across the floor. The hookah pipe on the side table had not been touched.

Maerko walked in through the inner side door, not the main door, with Saska at his heel and a flimsiplast in his right hand. He was already speaking. He was speaking to Saska, not to them.

"— and tell Petriv I want the secondary feed run hot for the next forty-eight hours, I don't care what it costs, I want every —" He saw them. He cut off. He stopped where he was. He looked at Saska. "Right. Hold that. I'll come back to you."

"Yes, lord."

He came around to his chair. He set the flimsiplast face down on the table. He sat down. He did not sit down all the way. He sat on the front half of the chair, both forearms on the table, and he looked at the four of them with an expression that, for the first time since they had walked in, did not contain a small private joke.

"I apologize," he said.

He said it without performance. He said it the way a man said it when he had been raised to think the word *apology* meant something and had not had the meaning trained out of him by power.

"That was an hour you should not have spent on a balcony. I had a situation that needed me in person. I'd like to tell you it won't happen again. Probably it will. I will try to keep it short."

"Acknowledged," Jag said.

"Good." Maerko inhaled. Held it. Let it out. He glanced at the table. He glanced at the teapot. He noticed, Jaina realized with a small cold finger along her spine, that the teapot was a new one. Fresh tray. Fresh cups. He looked, very briefly, at the small inner door at the side of the room — the one through which the aides had come in — and the look was not surprise. It was the look of a man clocking the timestamp on a piece of work that had been done well.

He turned back to them.

"Where were we," he said. "Seven sectors."

"Lord Maerko." Jaina set both hands flat on the table. "Before we continue. Will you indulge me a question."

"I'll indulge you several."

"The depot."

He looked at her, mild.

"The depot," he repeated, the way a man repeated a word he wanted to give himself a moment with.

"You toured one of your weapons depots."

"I did."

"In the middle of a state meeting."

"I did, Jedi Knight Solo. I told you I would not pretend the dance. The situation needed me on site. It is now resolved. I am sitting back down."

"What was in the depot."

A pause.

He looked at her for a long moment. He looked at Master Bel. He looked at Jag. His mouth moved in the not-quite-smile.

"You want a number," he said.

"I want a category."

"All right." He spread his hands on the table. They were broad, blunt-fingered, scarred along the knuckles. He spread them as if to demonstrate that he was hiding nothing in them. "Category. Several. The first category is conventional ordinance. Blasters, slugthrowers, the usual. That isn't what was being signed off today. That isn't worth me leaving a meeting for."

"What was."

"The second category." He picked up his teacup. He looked into it. He set it down. "Discontinued Imperial stockpile."

"Specifically."

"Specifically — and I'm going to give you this straight because frankly we will get to it faster — I have, in three secured sites across these sectors, a working inventory of chemical and radiological munitions, some of which we found, some of which we bought, and some of which we are restoring to full operability. Nerve agents from the last Bothan suppression. Three classes of fission ordinance from the old Sienar yards. A pair of binary contact agents that I personally believe should not exist and which I keep mostly because if I destroyed them somebody else would dig up another set."

He paused. He picked up his cup again. He drank.

"I also have," he added, almost as an afterthought, "a quantity of older stock that we recovered from a Mandalorian cache near Concord Dawn. Nothing major. Some neutron warheads. A handful of fissionables that were sitting in there for about four thousand standard years. They were, when we found them, decayed past usefulness. We have been restoring them with tibanna and a fair amount of patient work. The Mandalorians, as I understand it, are aware. They are not, as I understand it, sentimental."

Nobody at the table spoke.

Maerko set the cup down.

"This is why I went," he said. "The site superintendent at the third site would not sign off on a containment seal without my eyes on the door. He's a careful man. I pay him to be a careful man. He was right not to sign off. I went. We sealed it. I came back. That is the entire substance of the last hour, and that is also, Jedi Knight Solo, the reason I keep these stockpiles."

He laced his fingers together on the table.

He looked at her.

"They are insurance," he said pleasantly. "Against the day someone in the Alliance decides that the way to resolve our negotiations is by fleet. I am telling you about them, in this room, on this afternoon, because I want them factored into the conversation we are about to have. I'm not threatening you. Threats are vulgar. I am telling you what is on the table, on my side, alongside the seven sectors and the trade lanes and the people in this building who, by the way, would also rather not be evaporated."

A small silence.

Jag was the first to speak. His voice was perfectly level.

"You are telling us," Jag said, "that if the Alliance moves against you militarily, you will use weapons of mass destruction on Alliance forces."

"I am telling you," Maerko said, "that I have them. What I do with them depends on what is done to me. I have been at the receiving end of orbital bombardments, Lord Fel. I was on the *ground* during three of them. I do not enjoy the idea of one being conducted in my direction. I have arranged matters so that anyone who tries it will at least have a complicated conversation with their own staff afterward."

"And the use of these weapons offensively?"

"No interest." Maerko's mouth did the not-quite-smile. "What would I do with them. I'm building a thing here. You don't build a thing by salting it."

"Then their purpose —"

"Their purpose, Lord Fel, is to be loud when I want them to be loud. The loudness is, currently, about as loud as I want it. The day they go off is the day I have already lost the argument, and the day after that is the day my staff finds a new employer. Nobody wants that day. *I* don't want that day."

He paused.

He looked, very briefly, at Jaina.

"That said," he added, in a tone that was almost conversational, almost an aside, almost a thing he had not quite meant to say out loud and might or might not be performing, "I will admit a small personal curiosity. About the close-in flash effect. Whether the human body genuinely turns to ash at three hundred meters, or whether it's mostly a vapor problem. I've read both versions. I would not, for the avoidance of doubt, ever conduct an experiment to find out."

He paused another beat.

"Unless the experiment came to me," he said.

He smiled, briefly. The first real smile Jaina had seen from him. It did not reach the eyes. It did not need to.

"All the more reason," he said, picking his teacup up, "for your people to leave me alone."

He sipped.

He set the cup down.

"Now," he said. "The seven sectors."

